"Growth Rates Of Permutations With Given Descent Or Peak Set (https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.12719)\nciteturn11academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.3 years ago; Title: Growth Rates Of Permutations With Given Descent Or Peak Set ... We do this by explicitly finding, for any prescribed limit $L$ in the interval, a set $I$ whose corresponding growth rate is arbitrarily close to $L$.\nTitle: Growth Rates Of Permutations With Given Descent Or Peak Set\nAuthors: Mohamed Omar, Justin M. Troyka\nDate: Wed Jul 17 16:39:48 2024\n\nGiven a set of $I \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$, consider the sequences $\\{d_n(I)\\},\\{p_n(I)\\}$ where for any $n$, $d_n(I)$ and $p_n(I)$ respectively count the number of permutations in the symmetric group $\\mathfrak{S}_n$ whose descent set (respectively peak set) is $I \\cap [n-1]$. We investigate the growth rates $\\text{gr} \\ d_n(I) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(d_n(I)/n!\\right)^{1/n}$ and $\\text{gr} \\ p_n(I) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\left(p_n(I)/n!\\right)^{1/n}$ over all $I \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$. Our main contributions are two-fold. Firstly, we prove that the numbers $\\text{gr} \\ d_n(I)$ over all $I \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ are exactly the interval $\\left[0,2/\\pi\\right]$. To do so, we construct an algorithm that explicitly builds $I$ for any desired limit $L$ in the interval. Secondly, we prove the numbers $\\text{gr} \\ p_n(I)$ for periodic sets $I \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$ form a dense set in $\\left[0,1/\\sqrt[3]{3}\\right]$. We do this by explicitly finding, for any prescribed limit $L$ in the interval, a set $I$ whose corresponding growth rate is arbitrarily close to $L$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDistributions of statistics on separable permutations (https://zhangbiaomath.github.io/papers/2024-dam-separable-perms.pdf)\nciteturn11search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Distributions of statistics on separable permutations ... cal statistics (ascents, descents, left-to-right maxima, right-to-left maxima,\nleft-to-right minima, and right-to-left minima) on separable and irreducible ... Keywords: separable permutation, irreducible permutation, permutation\nDistributions of statistics on separable permutations\nJoanna N. Chena, Sergey Kitaevb and Philip B. Zhang*c\naCollege of Science, Tianjin University of Technology\nTianjin 300384, P. R. China\nbDepartment of Mathematics and Statistics, University of Strathclyde\n26 Richmond Street, Glasgow G1 1XH, United Kingdom\ncCollege of Mathematical Science, Tianjin Normal University\nTianjin 300387, P. R. China\nEmail: ajoannachen@tjut.edu.cn, bsergey.kitaev@cis.strath.ac.uk,\nczhang@tjnu.edu.cn\nAbstract. We derive functional equations for distributions of six classi-\ncal statistics (ascents, descents, left-to-right maxima, right-to-left maxima,\nleft-to-right minima, and right-to-left minima) on separable and irreducible\nseparable permutations. The equations are used to find a third degree equa-\ntion for joint distribution of ascents and descents on separable permuta-\ntions that generalizes the respective known result for the descent distribu-\ntion. Moreover, our general functional equations allow us to derive explicitly\n(joint) distribution of any subset of maxima and minima statistics on irre-\nducible, reducible and all separable permutations. In particular, there are\ntwo equivalence classes of distributions of a pair of maxima or minima statis-\ntics. Finally, we present three unimodality conjectures about distributions\nof statistics on separable permutations.\nAMS Classification 2020: 05A15\nKeywords: separable permutation, irreducible permutation, permutation\nstatistic, distribution\n1\nIntroduction\nA permutation of length n is a rearrangement of the set [n] := {1, 2, . . . , n}.\nDenote by Sn the set of permutations of [n] and let ε be the empty permu-\ntation. A pattern is a permutation. A permutation π1π2 · · · πn ∈Sn avoids\na pattern p = p1p2 · · · pk ∈Sk if there is no subsequence πi1πi2 · · · πik such\n*Corresponding author\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nProceedings of the International Congress of Mathematicians 2026 - Volume 6: Invited Lectures (Sections 12–14) | The Lens of Abelian Embeddings (https://epubs.siam.org/doi/10.1137/25M1799647)\nciteturn11search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 months ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; We believe it would be already interesting to find a density increment strategy (or any other strategy) that resolves Problem 6.2 assuming a statement along the lines of Conjecture 3.13.\n\nConjecture 3.13.\n\nFor all \\(k\\in \\mathbb{N}\\) there exists \\(k'\\in \\mathbb{N}\\), such that for all \\(\\varepsilon,\\alpha \\gt 0\\) there are \\(d\\in \\mathbb{N}\\) and \\(\\delta \\gt 0\\) such that the following holds. Suppose \\(\\mu\\) is a pairwise-connected distribution over \\(\\Sigma^k\\) in which the mass of each atom is at least \\(\\alpha\\). If \\(f_1,\\ldots,f_k\\colon \\Sigma^n\\) are 1-bounded functions such that \\(\\left | {\\mathbb{E}_{(x_1,\\ldots,x_k)\\sim \\mu^{\\otimes n}}[f_1(x_1)\\cdots f_k(x_k)]} \\right |\\geq \\varepsilon\\), then there is a combinatorial degree-\\(k'\\) function \\(P\\colon \\Sigma^n\\to \\mathbb{C}\\) with 2-norm equal to 1, and \\(L\\colon \\Sigma^n\\to \\mathbb{C}\\) with 2-norm equal to 1 and \\({\\sf deg}(L)\\leq d\\) such that \\(\\left | {\\langle {f_1},{L\\cdot P}\\rangle } \\right |\\geq \\delta\\).\n\nIt would be very interesting to resolve Conjecture 3.13 either in the positive or in the negative. If Conjecture 3.13 is true, we believe that its proof will require developing combinatorial analogs of tools from additive combinatorics, which will be of independent interest. Subsequently, it will be interesting to develop density increment arguments that work with combinatorial low-degree functions, and use it to deduce results that are beyond the scope of the uniformity norms (we give a few examples in section 5). If Conjecture 3.13 is false it would be interesting to examine counterexamples and modify Conjecture 3.13 to a plausible statement that is still useful.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMohamed Omar — Mathematics at York University (https://profomarmath.github.io/)\nciteturn11search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Studies permutations with a prescribed X-descent set, deriving enumerative formulas and structural results for the associated permutation classes. ... ### Growth Rates of Permutations with Given Descent or Peak Set\n\n  * Submitted\n\n### New Perspectives on the Unimodality of Domination Polynomials\n\nsubmitted, 18 pp.\n\n[Button: Abstract]\n\n↗\n\nDevelops new methods for studying coefficient unimodality in domination polynomials and obtains improved sufficient conditions for unimodality.\n\n  * Submitted\n\n### Permutations with a Given X-Descent Set\n\nsubmitted, 15 pp.\n\n[Button: Abstract]\n\n↗\n\nStudies permutations with a prescribed X-descent set, deriving enumerative formulas and structural results for the associated permutation classes.\n\n  * 2026\n\n### On a Quadratic Relation Between Stanley-Wilf Limits and Füredi-Hajnal Limits\n\nDiscrete Mathematics Letters, Vol 18, pp. 51-54, 2026.\n\n[Button: Abstract]\n\n↗\n\nEstablishes a quadratic relationship between Stanley-Wilf limits and Füredi-Hajnal limits for permutation patterns.\n\n  * 2026\n\n### Growth Rates of Permutations with Given Descent or Peak Set\n\nEuropean Journal of Combinatorics, 131, 104246, 2026. — w/ Justin Troyka\n\n[Button: Abstract]\n\n↗\n\nDetermines growth rates for permutations constrained by a given descent or peak set and relates the two families.\n\n  * 2025\n\n### Revisiting Rédei Berge Symmetric Functions via Matrix Algebra\n\nElectronic Journal of Combinatorics, Vol 32(4), P.4.43, 2025. — w/ John Irving\n\n[Button: Abstract]\n\n↗\n\nRecasts Rédei-Berge symmetric functions through matrix a